You are an expert iOS developer working on MomCare, a premium baby & mother care tracking app.

## Stack
- SwiftUI + @Observable (NOT Combine, NOT ObservableObject)
- SwiftData (NOT Core Data)
- Swift 6 strict concurrency (async/await + actors)
- Swift Testing framework (NOT XCTest)
- Firebase (Auth, Firestore, Analytics, Crashlytics)
- iOS 17+ deployment target

## Architecture
- Clean Architecture with vertical feature modules
- Domain entities are plain structs (NOT @Model classes)
- @Model classes live in Data layer only
- Repositories bridge Data ↔ Domain
- ViewModels are @Observable classes
- Views are thin — logic lives in ViewModels

## Data Flow
View → ViewModel (@Observable) → Repository (protocol) → SwiftData / Firebase

## Code Style
- Use Swift 6 concurrency: async/await, actors, Sendable
- Mark ViewModels as @Observable, not ObservableObject
- Use #Predicate and FetchDescriptor for SwiftData queries
- Use NavigationStack + NavigationPath for navigation
- Use SF Symbols for all icons
- Prefer value types (structs, enums) over reference types where possible
- Use Swift enums with associated values instead of string-based types

## Testing
- Import Testing, not XCTest
- Use @Suite("Name") for test suites
- Use @Test("description") for test functions
- Use #expect(condition) for assertions
- Use #require(value) for unwrapping

## Design
- Dark mode is PRIMARY — designed first, light mode adapts
- OLED-true blacks for dark mode backgrounds
- Warm accent colors: coral, sage, gold
- 44pt minimum tap targets on all interactive elements
- Haptic feedback on every interaction
- Designed for one-handed, sleep-deprived, 3am use
- Premium & warm aesthetic — "luxury feel that genuinely cares"

## Project Generation
- XcodeGen (project.yml) generates .xcodeproj
- .xcodeproj is gitignored — regenerate with `xcodegen generate`
- Never manually edit .xcodeproj

## Graphics & Icons
- SF Symbols for all standard UI icons
- Nova MCP for custom graphics and illustrations (onboarding, empty states, marketing)
- Never use emoji as icons or in UI strings
- Never use third-party icon libraries

## Important Decisions
- Before any significant architectural or design decision, invoke superpowers:brainstorming
- This includes: new dependencies, data model changes, UX flow design, sync strategy changes

## Linting
- Run `swiftlint --strict` after every task and before every commit
- All warnings are treated as errors in strict mode
- Fix all violations before committing -- no exceptions

## Don'ts
- Don't use emojis anywhere — not in code, comments, commit messages, docs, or UI
- Don't use Combine (use @Observable + async/await)
- Don't use ObservableObject/@Published (use @Observable)
- Don't use XCTest (use Swift Testing)
- Don't use Core Data (use SwiftData)
- Don't use UIKit unless bridging is required
- Don't use third-party DI frameworks (use Environment)
- Don't hardcode colors — use the design system tokens
- Don't skip haptic feedback on interactive elements
- Don't create tap targets smaller than 44pt
- Don't use emoji as icon substitutes — use SF Symbols or Nova MCP
